## 2. The core design rule: no shared join key

`internal_ledger` (your `transactions` / ledger source) and
`processor_settlement` (your `settlement` source) share **no identifier**.
Each has its own independent ID:

- `internal_record_id` — e.g. `INT-HISTORICAL-000010`
- `processor_record_id` — e.g. `SET-HISTORICAL-000010`

The only thing tying a ledger record to its true counterpart is
`label_group_id`, and that field exists **only** in `*_ground_truth.csv` /
`.json` — never in the two business collections. This is deliberate and
important: it means a reconciliation agent built against this data has to
actually match on amount / date / reference / customer / currency, the way
the blueprint's Reconcile node is specified to work (p.16). If you load
`internal_ledger` and `processor_settlement` into Mongo as-is, there is no
column a matcher could cheat with — it has to do real work.

Use `*_ground_truth` only in your backtester/evaluator, exactly as the
blueprint's `label_group_id` is described (p.9): *"hidden from agent, used
by evaluator."*

---

## 3. Record schema

**`internal_ledger`** (ledger source):
```
internal_record_id   str    e.g. "INT-HISTORICAL-000010"
event_time           str    ISO datetime
merchant_id          str    one of MRC_001..MRC_005
customer_id          str    e.g. "CUST-0301" (pool of 400)
processor             str    one of PROC_A, PROC_B, PROC_C
channel               str    card | ach | wire | wallet
country               str    US (x3 weight) | CA | GB | DE
amount_cents          int    integer cents — avoids float rounding at the 1-5¢ tolerance level
currency              str    USD (x4 weight) | EUR | GBP
settlement_date        str    ISO date
reference              str    e.g. "PAY-100010"
status                 str    "posted"
```

**`processor_settlement`** (settlement source): same shape, with
`processor_record_id` / `received_time` / `status: "settled"` in place of
the ledger-only fields. `amount_cents`, `currency`, `settlement_date`, or
`reference` diverge from the ledger record depending on which break
pattern was applied (see below); everything else (merchant, customer,
processor, channel, country) matches for genuine pairs.

**`ground_truth`** (evaluator-only):
```
label_group_id                str    hidden truth key — never in the two business tables
dataset_split                  str    "historical" | "live"
live_phase                     str    "" | "baseline" | "fee_drift" | "settlement_drift"
pattern                        str    the break-pattern name (see table below)
is_true_match                  int    1 if this pair should auto/eventually resolve, 0 if it never should
anomaly_reason                 str    short cause label
expected_rule                  str    which policy mechanism should resolve it
fee_cents                      int    fee delta applied (processing_fee pattern only)
delay_days                     int    delay applied (settlement_delay pattern only)
expected_internal_record_id    str    for scoring a matcher's output
expected_processor_record_id   str    for scoring a matcher's output
```

---

## 4. Break patterns

| Pattern | Historical share | What changes | `is_true_match` | `expected_rule` |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `exact_match` | 68% | nothing — identical amount/date/reference/customer | 1 | `exact` |
| `processing_fee` | 10% | settlement amount is 1–5¢ lower | 1 | `amount_tolerance` |
| `settlement_delay` | 8% | settlement date is T+1 or T+2 | 1 | `date_tolerance` |
| `reference_format` | 6% | reference reformatted (no dashes / lowercase / `-SETTLED` suffix / `P/` prefix) | 1 | `reference_normalization` |
| `rounding_difference` | 3% | settlement amount is ±1¢ | 1 | `amount_tolerance` |
| `true_amount_mismatch` | 2.5% | settlement amount differs by $0.50–$25.00 | **0** | `do_not_match` |
| `duplicate_processor_record` | 1.5% | an extra processor record is inserted with the same details plus a `-DUP` suffix ID | 1 (original) | `dedupe_then_match` |
| `currency_mismatch` | 1% | settlement currency swapped to a different one | **0** | `do_not_match` |

The two `is_true_match: 0` patterns are your **negative examples** —
near-identical pairs that a too-loose policy could wrongly auto-match. They
exist specifically to make `false_match_rate` a meaningful, non-zero-risk
metric during backtesting (blueprint p.20–21), rather than a number that's
trivially always zero.

`duplicate_processor_record` adds one extra row to
`historical_processor_settlement` beyond the 1:1 pairing — same
`processor_record_id` root plus `-DUP`, arriving 7 seconds after the
original. This is a dedupe scenario: your matcher needs to collapse two
processor records into one match, not a mismatch pattern.

