## 5. Historical split (8,000 pairs → 8,115 processor rows)

Flat distribution across all 3 processors, static pattern weights for the
whole run (table above). This is your **backtesting corpus** — feed it to
`evaluate_policy()` (blueprint p.20) to score `policy_v1` vs. candidate
policies across the full range of break types, including the two
negative-example patterns.

## 6. Live split (1,500 rows) — three-phase demo narrative

| Phase | Rows | Story |
|---|---|---|
| `baseline` | 0–399 | Normal traffic — 82% exact match, small trickle of every break type across all 3 processors |
| `fee_drift` | 400–999 | `processing_fee` share jumps to 35%, and **every one of those cases is pinned to processor `PROC_B` with a fixed 3¢ fee** — a clean, diagnosable cluster |
| `settlement_drift` | 1000–1499 | `settlement_delay` share jumps to 22%, **every one of those cases pinned to processor `PROC_C` with a fixed 1-day delay** — the optional second incident from the blueprint (p.10) |

Pinning the drift patterns to one processor and one fixed value (rather
than sampling randomly from `[1,2,3,5]¢`) was a deliberate fix: it makes
the blueprint's deterministic anomaly trigger — *"≥10 unresolved cases with
the same signed delta in ≥70% of them"* (p.10) — actually fire cleanly.
Verified in this run: **218/218 (100%)** of `fee_drift` processing-fee
cases carry exactly 3¢ on `PROC_B`, and **103/103 (100%)** of
`settlement_drift` delay cases carry exactly 1 day on `PROC_C`. Your
Diagnosis agent will have a genuinely clean, nameable pattern to find —
"processor PROC_B, systematic −3¢" — matching the blueprint's own demo
script language (p.37).

