## 7. Seed policy and candidates

`policy_v1.json` — the ACTIVE policy your reconciler starts with (zero
tolerance on everything, so historical/live break-pattern records fail to
auto-match until a candidate is promoted):

```json
{
  "version": "v1",
  "status": "active",
  "matching": {
    "amount_tolerance_cents": 0,
    "date_tolerance_days": 0,
    "normalize_reference": false,
    "require_same_currency": true,
    "require_same_customer": true
  },
  "guardrails": {
    "max_false_match_rate": 0.005,
    "min_backtest_records": 1000,
    "require_human_approval": true
  }
}
```

`candidate_policy_examples.json` has two example evolutions:
`candidate_fee_v2` (5¢ tolerance — should resolve the fee-drift cases) and
`candidate_settlement_v3` (adds 1-day date tolerance + reference
normalization — should additionally resolve the settlement-drift cases).
Field names now match the blueprint's allowlisted Evolution-agent schema
exactly (`amount_tolerance_cents`, range 0–25, p.19), so you can feed these
straight in as example candidates without a units conversion step.

`incident_memories.csv` — 4 seed long-term-memory rows matching the
`incident_memories` collection shape from p.13 (fee tolerance, T+1 delay,
reference normalization, and a hard guardrail entry for large-delta/currency
mismatches). These are generic seed knowledge, not tied to measured numbers
from this specific dataset — once your matcher and backtester exist, you'll
want to compute real before/after metrics from a promoted policy and store
those as the "outcome" the way the blueprint's `MEM-17` example does
(p.13, "+22% auto-resolution").

